1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Strategy: Our pawn chain points to the kingside, our
side of the board, where we would like to attack. It's our side because it's
easier for us to transfer force (pieces) there. This means that we are ok with
making weaknesses there, for we have the juice to defend them. And we want black
to give us something (anything $1) there (on the kingside) for us to sink our
teeth into. Black's pawns point to the queenside. Black will need to make drama
there and against our d4 point to avoid getting mated. Making things easy: Like
our 3.e5 vs the Caro we dramatically limit black's options. By contrast the
traditional 3. Nc3 allows the Winawer (Bb4) and Steinitz (Nf6) both of those
will give black the kind of trench warfare that encouraged them to play the
French in the first place. 3. Nd2 allows even more options, with 3...c5; 3...Nf6
and even 3...Be7. Style: For hundreds of years white players have tried to make
an attacking formation with Nf3 and Bd3 work. It's now possible. } 3... c5 4. c3 Nc6 (4... Qb6 5. Nf3 Bd7 { intending to trade his bad bishop for ours at the cost
