the board, exactly where you, the black king, are dreaming of hiding. That is
why white must foresee black wanting f6 at some point. That break will give
black the option of defending their king laterally, with say the queen on c7 or
a rook on f7. Therefore our overprotection of e5 isn't just about protecting a
pawn, we want our force to be ready to flow through that square when black does
play f6. You can think of e5 as the sharp point of our spear. } 8... Nge7 (8... f6 $6 { This move usually
